eplacing them with a single uniform population. Initially, both L0 and S0 populations are set to a tensor of zeros with the same shape as the input X. L and S update gates GLt and G S t (same shape as L and S) are computed as functions of the input and current hidden states Lt\u22121 and St\u22121 using 1x1 convolutions U\u2217\u2217.\nGLt = \u03c3(LN(Ulx \u2217X) + LN(Ull \u2217 Lt\u22121)) (1)\nGSt = \u03c3(LN(Usx \u2217X) + LN(Uss \u2217 St\u22121)) (2)\nEach of the 4 types of lateral connections namely 1) L \u2212\u2192 L, 2) L \u2212\u2192 S, 3) S \u2212\u2192 S, 4) S \u2212\u2192 L neurons are modeled by convolution kernels Wll,Wsl,Wss, and Wls respectively. Wll and Wls modeling long-range lateral interactions are of shape d\u00d7 d\u00d7 hl \u00d7wl where d is the dimensionality of the hidden state and hl and wl represent the kernel spatial size. To be precise, each neuron at location (i, j,m) in L (spatial location i,j and channel m) receives lateral connections from a window of neurons at locations (x, y, n), x \u2208 [i\u2212 hl/2, i+ hl/2], y \u2208 [j \u2212 wl/2, j + wl/2], n \u2208 [0, k) in L and S mediated by Wll and Wls respectively. Similarly, Wss,Wsl are of shape hs and ws such that (hl, wl) > (hs, ws).\nL\u0303t = \u03b3(Wlx \u2217X+Wll \u2217 Lt\u22121 +Wls \u2217 St\u22121) (3)\nS\u0303t = \u03b3(Wsx \u2217X+Wsl \u2217 Lt\u22121 +Wss \u2217 St\u22121) (4)\nOnce the long-range lateral influences are computed and stored in L\u0303t and S\u0303t, these are mixed with the previous hidden states using the gates computed in Eq. 1. These hidden states are then passed on to subsequent recurrent iterations where even longer-range interactions occur (as time increases).\nLt = \u03ba(LN(G L t \u2299 L\u0303t + (1\u2212GLt )\u2299 Lt\u22121)) (5)\nSt = \u03ba(LN(G S t \u2299 S\u0303t + (1\u2212GSt )\u2299 St\u22121)) (6)\nIn the above equations, LN() stands for Layer Normalization (Ba et al., 2016), and the nonlinearities \u03b3 and \u03ba are both set to 